    {
      "title": "From scrap dealer to FBI's most-wanted drug lord: Who is El Chapo Isidro?",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/from-scrap-dealer-to-fbi-s-most-wanted-drug-lord-who-is-el-chapo-isidro-126112200053_1.html",
      "description": "Fausto Isidro Meza Flores rose from a small-scale Mexican drug trafficker to one of the FBI's most wanted international drug lords before his detention, which came after the agency said he spent years trafficking heroin, cocaine, fentanyl and other drugs to the US.\nMeza Flores, known as \"El Chapo Isidro\", was captured on Friday night into Saturday by Mexican authorities aided by intelligence from the Trump administration.\nThe 44-year-old Meza Flores was one of the FBI's top 10 most wanted fugitives, with a USD 5 million reward for information leading to his arrest.\nHis history of drug trafficking stretches back more than 20 years and saw him lead the violent Beltran Leyva organisation and later his own group, the Meza-Flores Transnational Criminal Organisation, according to the FBI and Mexico's government. Beyond drug trafficking, the group -- also known as Los Mazatlecos -- is accused of kidnapping, extortion, torture and murder, the agency has said.\nMeza Flores is allied with a .",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-22T02:05:20"
    },
    {
      "title": "Mexico captures top cartel boss El Chapo Isidro, violence erupts in Sinaloa",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/mexico-captures-top-cartel-boss-el-chapo-isidro-violence-erupts-in-sinaloa-126112200014_1.html",
      "description": "Gunfire rang out, trucks burned and Mexican forces clashed with cartel gunmen in the state of Sinaloa overnight as Mexican authorities captured cartel boss Fausto Isidro Meza Flores, known as \"El Chapo Isidro.\"\nMeza Flores was one of the FBI's 10 most wanted fugitives. He climbed the cartel ranks to lead the Beltran Leyva cartel and later became the leader of his own group, the Meza-Flores Transnational Criminal Organization. The United States had a USD 5 million reward offer for information leading to his arrest, and he was wanted for trafficking drugs to the US and illegally carrying firearms.\nMexico's government said in a report early Saturday that intelligence from the Trump administration helped authorities capture him, but provided few details about how authorities in Mexico and the United States coordinated to detain Meza Flores.\nHe was also a key ally to \"Los Mayos,\" a faction of the Sinaloa Cartel, which was designated as a foreign terrorist organization by the Trump ...",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-22T01:55:31"
    }
